Yes. In fact, this can become one of the strongest differentiators of your platform. Instead of simply asking “Is this story implemented?”, build an Implementation Confidence Engine that estimates how completely a story has been realized in the codebase.

The important design principle is:

Don’t ask the LLM to analyze the entire repository. Use static analysis to gather evidence, then ask the LLM to reason over that evidence.

This keeps the solution fast, inexpensive, auditable, and minimally intrusive.

⸻

High-Level Architecture

             Git Repository
                    │
          Incremental Repository Indexer
                    │
     ┌──────────────┼───────────────┐
     │              │               │
 Static Facts   Semantic Index   Call Graph
     │              │               │
     └──────────────┼───────────────┘
                    │
           Evidence Retrieval
                    │
          Story Implementation Engine
                    │
      Evidence + Story + Acceptance Criteria
                    │
          Bedrock (Reasoning Only)
                    │
      Confidence Score + Missing Items

The AI receives evidence, not raw source code.

