A Better Metric Than a Single Confidence Score

Instead of returning only “92%”, return an Implementation Readiness Report.

Implementation Confidence
92%
Coverage
█████████░ 90%
Acceptance Criteria
████████░░ 82%
Business Rules
██████████ 100%
Repository Evidence
█████████░ 88%
Testing
███████░░░ 72%
Documentation
█████░░░░░ 55%

Below the score, list:

* Acceptance criteria with no supporting evidence.
* Code elements that appear unrelated or incomplete.
* Suggested tests to increase confidence.
* Relevant commits and pull requests that contributed evidence.

This is significantly more actionable than a single percentage and provides reviewers with traceable justification.

Why this is minimally intrusive

For a banking environment, this approach is attractive because:

* The repository is analyzed locally without modifying source code.
* Most processing is deterministic static analysis, which is inexpensive compared to LLM inference.
* Only concise summaries and evidence are sent to Bedrock, reducing cost and avoiding unnecessary exposure of proprietary code.
* Incremental indexing means only changed files are reprocessed, making the system suitable for large monorepos and frequent commits.

Given your earlier design for StoryLens with JIRA, document context, and repository indexing, this implementation confidence engine fits naturally as another service that consumes the same knowledge graph and retrieval infrastructure rather than introducing a separate pipeline.
